WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:03.690
Abraham: In a time where almost half
of UK residents have purchased at

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:08.119
least one smart device since the start
of the covid19 pandemic, the threat

00:00:08.119 --> 00:00:11.360
to users and their networks from
cyber attacks has never been greater.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:16.490
Everyday products such as smart
watches, TV's cameras and other

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:20.860
smart appliances offer huge benefits
but are vulnerable to cyber attack.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:25.390
To counter this threat the Government
is planning a new law to make sure

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:28.779
that virtually all smart devices
meet new security requirements.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.539
Some of the main points
of the law will include.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:36.770
• “Customers must be informed at
the point of sale the duration of

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.500
time for which a smart device will
receive security software updates”

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:44.920
• A ban on manufacturers using
universal default passwords, such

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.260
as ‘password’ or ‘admin’, that are
often pre-set in a device’s factory

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.990
settings and are easily guessable

00:00:51.759 --> 00:00:54.259
• Manufacturers will be required
to provide a public point of

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.520
contact to make it simpler for
anyone to report a vulnerability.”

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.480
Smartphones are also to be covered by the
planned 'Secure by Design' legislation.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.189
Many people keep their phones for
more than four years whilst some

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:09.430
companies only offer security
updates for just over 2 years.

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:13.700
Security updates are important
in protecting users against cyber

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.020
criminals and tech firms need to be
more up front about how long they

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.660
support the security of devices.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.449
Tech associations including the Internet
of Secure Things [IoXT] which includes

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.200
large companies like Google, Amazon and
Facebook support this new legislation

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.220
to boost the security of smart devices.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.740
The government is also looking at research
reports providing further evidence:

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.529
Development for this legislation
has been by the Department for

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:44.720
Digital, Culture, Media and Sport
[DCMS], in conjunction with the

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.779
National Cyber Security Centre [NCSC]

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:52.220
“The aim of this Code of Practice is
to support all parties involved in the

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:57.550
development, manufacturing and retail
of consumer IoT with a set of guidelines

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.429
to ensure that products are secure by
design and to make it easier for people

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:03.429
to stay secure in a digital world.”

